# Counsel Culture — Project Report

## What this is

Counsel Culture is a live video app built around **structured debate**, not
open-ended talk. Every chat room a group hangs out in carries a paired
**debate room**: a side space where a formal, turn-based discussion happens
on a set topic, with an enforced speaking order, a visible timer, a live
transcript, and on-demand fact-checking of what's being said. Anyone in the
chat room can watch and follow the debate without joining it; anyone can
raise their hand to join the speaker queue.

This document explains the tools involved and how the pieces fit together,
so it can be read cold and give a working mental model of the system.

## Tech stack

| Layer | Tool | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Client | React 19 + Vite + TypeScript | UI and build tooling |
| Client routing | react-router-dom | Join screen → chat room → debate room navigation |
| Realtime media | **LiveKit** (self-hosted `livekit-server`, open source, Apache-2.0) | WebRTC SFU — handles all audio/video transport for both room types |
| Media client SDK | `livekit-client` + `@livekit/components-react` | Connects to LiveKit rooms, prebuilt video-grid UI, React hooks for participants/tracks/data messages |
| Backend | Express (Node.js, ESM) | Issues LiveKit join tokens, runs the debate state machine, REST API |
| Server-side LiveKit control | `livekit-server-sdk` (`AccessToken`, `RoomServiceClient`) | Mints signed join tokens; enforces mic permissions and broadcasts state server-side |
| Live transcription | Browser `SpeechRecognition` API (Chrome/Edge) | Dev-scale live captioning, see **Limitations** below |
| Fact-checking | Anthropic API (`@anthropic-ai/sdk`, Claude) | On-demand claim assessment against the live transcript |
| Hosting (current) | Oracle Cloud "Always Free" VM (Ubuntu 22.04), systemd services | Self-hosted, zero infra cost, stable public IP |

## The two-room model

Every chat room named `foo` has an associated debate room named `foo::debate`.
They are **two separate LiveKit rooms**, not one room with roles — this is
what makes "observe the debate without leaving chat" cheap and simple: a
chat-room participant's browser holds a second, subscribe-only connection to
the debate room purely to receive its state and render the current speaker,
without being a debate participant.

```
Join screen
     |
     v
 Chat room  (LiveKit room "foo")
     |  \
     |   \--- always-on, subscribe-only connection ---> Debate room ("foo::debate")
     |                                                        ^
     `-- [Debate] button --------------------------------------'
                                                    (full participant connection)
```

Three participant roles, encoded as LiveKit **token grants** (`server/src/index.js`):

- **`chat`** — normal full audio+video, used in the main chat room.
- **`debate-participant`** — camera allowed immediately; **microphone is not
  granted at token-issue time**. It's added dynamically, per-turn, by the
  server (see below). Used when someone clicks "Debate."
- **`debate-observer`** — subscribe-only, can't publish anything. Used by the
  chat room's always-connected side panel.

## The debate state machine

`server/src/debateEngine.js` holds one `DebateEngine` instance per debate
room (in-memory, created on first use), and is the **single source of
truth** for: the topic, the ordered speaker queue, whose turn it is, and the
transcript. Clients never decide any of this themselves — they call REST
endpoints, and the engine reacts.

**Turn cycle** (90s per speaker by default):
1. `phase: "speaking"` — current speaker has the mic, `turnEndsAt` counts down.
2. When the timer lapses, `phase: "handoff"` for exactly 5 seconds — this is
   the `microphone handoff : {current} --> {next}` banner.
3. Queue advances, next speaker's turn begins, back to `speaking`.
4. If the queue is empty, `phase: "waiting"` until someone raises their hand.

**How the mic is actually enforced** (not just UI convention): the token
grants everyone `canPublishSources: ["camera"]` only. When it becomes a
participant's turn, the server calls
`RoomServiceClient.updateParticipant(room, identity, { permission: { canPublishSources: ["camera", "microphone"] } })`
— a live, server-side permission change LiveKit pushes to that already-connected
client. When their turn ends, the server revokes it the same way. A client
can't just unmute out of turn; LiveKit itself will refuse to publish an
unauthorized microphone track. The client also cooperatively calls
`localParticipant.setMicrophoneEnabled()` in sync with this so the local mic
UI matches, but the server-side permission is what's actually authoritative.

**State delivery**: the server pushes every state change to the room over
LiveKit's built-in **reliable data channel** (`RoomServiceClient.sendData`,
topic `"debate-state"` for state, `"debate-transcript"` for transcript lines
and fact-check results). Every connected client — full debate participants
*and* chat-room observers — receives the same broadcast over the same
transport already used for media, so there's no separate WebSocket
infrastructure to run or scale. A client that joins mid-debate hydrates via
plain REST (`GET /debate/:room/state`, `GET /debate/:room/transcript`) first,
then stays live off the data channel.

## Live transcription and fact-checking

- **Transcription**: while it's a participant's turn, their own browser runs
  the native `SpeechRecognition` API and POSTs each finalized utterance to
  `POST /debate/:room/transcript`. The server appends it to that room's
  transcript (in memory) and broadcasts it to everyone.
- **Fact-checking**: anyone (participant or chat-room observer) can click
  "Fact-check" on any transcript line. This calls
  `POST /debate/:room/fact-check/:lineId`, which sends the statement to
  Claude with a constrained prompt and stores/broadcasts a structured verdict
  (`verdict`, `explanation`, `confidence`). Fact-checking is inert (returns a
  clear "not configured" response) unless `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set in
  `server/.env` — nothing breaks without it, the feature just stays off.
- **The record**: `GET /debate/:room/transcript` returns the full transcript
  with any fact-check verdicts attached, so "access to the record" is just an
  HTTP call away for anyone, live or after the fact (until the server
  restarts — see Limitations).

