## Deployment

Runs on a single Oracle Cloud Always Free VM today: `livekit-server` and the
Express app both run as systemd services (`livekit.service`,
`counsel-culture.service`), so they survive reboots and SSH disconnects. The
Express server also serves the built client (`client/dist`) directly, so the
whole app is one process on one port from the outside — no separate static
host needed. See `server/.env.example` and `livekit/livekit.example.yaml` for
the config shape (the real files hold secrets and are gitignored).

## Known limitations / next steps

These are deliberate scope cuts for a working first version, not oversights:

- **State is in-memory, per-process.** Debate state and transcripts vanish on
  server restart, and this can't yet run as more than one server instance
  (a second instance would have its own, disconnected copy of every debate's
  state). Fixing this — moving state into Redis/Postgres — is exactly the
  multi-server scaling step described for handling hundreds of concurrent
  users.
- **Transcription is browser-native (Chrome/Edge only), not server-side.**
  Fine for a live prototype; a production version should move this to a
  proper STT service (self-hosted Whisper, or a low-latency API) so it works
  in any browser and doesn't depend on the speaker's own device.
- **No moderator/host role yet.** Anyone can set the topic or join the
  speaker queue. Roles, admission control, and the "verify a stream key
  before going live" concept from the original design are the natural next
  layer on top of the token-issuance code.
- **Turn duration (90s) is a constant**, not configurable per debate yet.
